.SS "template<uint32_t dim> \fBcsmp::Index\fP \fBcsmp::PropertyDatabase\fP< dim >::AddProperty ()"

.PP
adding a property at runtime from the command line (NB: you should call method CreateProperty() of model to do this) Interactive method allowing to a add a property at runtime, provided that the user knows which storage-vector index he can assign (this can be established by first calling ScalarProperties() or an equivalent method to establish how many properties of the desired type there already are\&. The index of the new property should use the next higher integer such that the property can be allocated by the MemoryManager at the end of the current property storage\&.

.PP
\fBWarning\fP
.RS 4
Any runtime change in variables invalidates existing \fBIndex\fP objects! They need to be updated\&.
.RE
.PP
.SH "Input Arguments"
.PP
The user will be prompted to enter the \fBParameter\fP record specifications\&.

.PP
A \fBcsmp::Index\fP for the new property\&.
.SH "Implementation"
.PP
The method will first prompt the user to define the characteristics of the new variable\&. Then CountVariables() is called to update the variable number lists inside of the \fBPropertyDatabase\fP\&. Finally, the updated variable list will be written to file\&.
.SH "Application Examples"
.PP
To define a new variable at runtime\&.
.SH "Messages"
.PP
The method will report a warning and print the existing variable record if the new variable already exists in the property database\&.

.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
The \fBIndex\fP reflecting the new state ov \fBLocalVariables\fP and \fBIntegrationPointVariables\fP, including added property\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBTodo\fP
.RS 4
(3-D) These cout should become either \fBException\fP or errors using \fBErrorHandler\fP 

.PP
(3-D) Refactor to use overload internally 
.RE
.PP
Roman,2013: Added explicit way of reading the size of variable provided by user input in function added_prop\&.DefineFromStdin()
.SS "template<uint32_t dim> bool \fBcsmp::PropertyDatabase\fP< dim >::CheckRange (const char * s, const \fBScalarVariable\fP & var) const"

.PP
checks value against the range specified in the database; 
.PP
\fBNote\fP
.RS 4
not fast; use only for selected values
.RE
.PP
returns true if tha value of the supplied variable is within the range stored in the database, else false 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim> void \fBcsmp::PropertyDatabase\fP< dim >::CheckRange (const char * s, double & var) const"

.PP
prints details of the range check to screen and terminates program is value is out of range Tests whether the supplied value of the target variable is within the physical meaningful range which was specified for the variable in the database file\&. 
.br

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIs\fP The first method argument give the name of the target variable\&.
.br
\fIvar\fP The second method argument specifies the actual physical-variable value which will be overwritten by the closest range bound if the target variable is out of range\&.
.RE
.PP
.SH "Implementation"
.PP
Checks range of variable *s\&. A warning is issued if the variable is out of range and the variable is set to the closest range bound\&.
.SH "Messages"
.PP
If the target variable has not been defined in the property database, an error will be reported\&. 
